From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:17:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] editor overlay: slope view blocks and coarse node grids are
 draped over the real ground, "in front of the camera" uses the clip w, block
 level with hysteresis

- EdOverlayDrape.h: DrapePatch cuts a patch 2 x 2 (depth <= 4, single cells, quad budget) where the true projection of the edge midpoints / centre is more than 0.75 px
  from the perspective-correct flat patch through the corners (a screen space bilinear would split flat ground seen at a low angle). Slope blocks (60000 quads per
  frame) and runs of coarse node grids (30000) go through it; the slope view keeps its quads while camera, heights, view and colours stay the same.
- the test "point is in front of the camera" was viewport.r[1] . p >= 1: row 1 is the LOD plane (world width of the view at that depth * camera lodScale), so it moved with
  cam_SetLOD / the LOD options (0 = nothing drawn, 0.1 = nothing nearer than ~5 units). Now the clip w of viewProjection (what Project divides by) > kNearW, everywhere in EdOverlay.cpp.
- LevelWithHysteresis: the block level goes coarser at 1.25 x the block limit and finer at 0.8 x, so zooming or a jittering view size no longer flips it.
- tests: EdOverlayDrapeTest (new), EdOverlaySlopeTest (hysteresis).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01F37er45a7etRuMgEW1q3kY

